+          "raw_response": "{\n  \"headline\": \"Why Are Physical Menus and Printed Zines Becoming Status Symbols in a Digital Age?\",\n  \"dek\": \"From hand-drawn pie menus to collectible zines, the resurgence of print media and physical artifacts is capturing the imagination of a new generation.\",\n  \"body_markdown\": \"In an era dominated by digital screens and instant access, the physical world is making a comeback. This is evident in the growing popularity of printed zines and handcrafted menus, which are no longer just functional items but are becoming coveted collectibles. These tangible artifacts are being embraced by a generation that values authenticity, craftsmanship, and the tactile experience of print media.\\n\\nTake, for example, Quo Vadis, a restaurant that has made its menu a work of art. The 'Pie of the Day' is highlighted with a John Broadley illustration, turning a simple dish into a visual statement. Chef Jeremy Lee emphasizes the traditional value of pies, using all kitchen produce and scraps for stocks, reinforcing the idea that food, like print, is best when made with care and intention. This approach is also reflected in Lee’s book, *Cooking: Simply and Well, for One or Many*, where pies are celebrated with a variety of recipes that highlight seasonal and traditional British cuisine.\\n\\nMeanwhile, in the world of print media, zines are experiencing a renaissance. Hato Press recently launched its 50th zine with an exhibition showcasing all artist editions since 2020. These zines are Risograph-printed and part of an ongoing series with no creative constraints, allowing artists like Wilfrid Wood and Emma Larsson to express themselves freely. The exhibition also featured bespoke objects and artworks, reinforcing the idea that zines are not just publications but also collectible items with artistic value.\\n\\nThis trend is not limited to niche circles. i-D magazine, which had been absent since 2023 due to Vice's bankruptcy, has returned to UK newsstands, fueled by a renewed interest in luxury and indie titles. Acquired by Karlie Kloss through her Bedford Media conglomerate, the magazine's relaunch reflects a broader cultural shift toward print media, driven in part by Gen Z's nostalgia for physical artifacts.\\n\\nZines, in particular, have a cool factor that draws brands and influences publishing trends. Independent publications and zines like OOMK, The Chapess, and Diaspora Drama have long provided an alternative to mainstream glossy magazines, often created on shoestring budgets and through grassroots efforts. Their resurgence highlights a desire for authenticity and a rejection of mass-produced content.\\n\\nAs digital fatigue sets in, the appeal of physical items—whether a beautifully illustrated menu or a hand-printed zine—grows. These objects are not just functional; they are status symbols, representing a commitment to craftsmanship, creativity, and a connection to the past. In a world that moves at lightning speed, the tangible and the timeless are becoming increasingly valuable.\",\n  \"claim_to_source\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Quo Vadis features a 'Pie of the Day' with a fixed price of £19.50, highlighted on the menu with a John Broadley illustration.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"source:a3a957f59e18dd4b54904fa250f129af3927a572c0bda8e1fb4400a6c4ef298e:17f94a5e-25e8-4ae7-b1d7-add0303ad31e:2\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Jeremy Lee emphasizes the traditional value of pies, using all kitchen produce and scraps for stocks.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"source:a3a957f59e18dd4b54904fa250f129af3927a572c0bda8e1fb4400a6c4ef298e:17f94a5e-25e8-4ae7-b1d7-add0303ad31e:2\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Pies are highlighted in Lee's book 'Cooking: Simply and Well, for One or Many' with various recipes.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"source:a3a957f59e18dd4b54904fa250f129af3927a572c0bda8e1fb4400a6c4ef298e:17f94a5e-25e8-4ae7-b1d7-add0303ad31e:2\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Quo Vadis 