+  "raw_response": "{\"headline\": \"Coffee Consumption Linked to Lower Risk of Liver Cancer and Cirrhosis\", \"dek\": \"New research suggests that drinking five or more cups of coffee daily may significantly reduce the risk of liver cancer, cirrhosis, and other liver-related diseases.\", \"body_markdown\": \"### Coffee May Protect Against Liver Disease\\nA growing body of evidence indicates that regular coffee consumption is associated with a reduced risk of liver disease. A large-scale study conducted by UK Biobank tracked over 350,000 individuals for approximately 13 years and found that those who drank five or more cups of coffee daily had a **47% lower risk** of developing liver cancer compared to non-coffee drinkers.\\n\\nThis protective effect was also observed in decaffeinated coffee consumers, suggesting that compounds other than caffeine may be responsible for the benefits. Researchers noted that both caffeinated and decaf coffee were linked to healthier liver function markers, including lower levels of inflammation and scarring.\\n\\n### Liver Health Benefits Across Coffee Intake Levels\\nThe findings show that even moderate consumption can offer some protection. For instance:\\n- Drinking one to two cups per day is associated with a **20% reduction** in cirrhosis risk.\\n- Consuming five or more cups daily reduces the risk of cirrhosis by approximately **32%**, regardless of whether the coffee was caffeinated or not.\\n\\nThese results align with other studies showing that higher coffee intake correlates with significantly reduced risks of liver disease, liver cancer, and liver-related death.\\n\\n### Surprising Insight: Decaf Offers Similar Protection\\nOne surprising detail from recent research is that decaffeinated coffee provides nearly the same level of liver protection as regular coffee. This suggests that compounds such as polyphenols—found in both types of coffee—are likely responsible for these health benefits rather than caffeine itself.\\n\\n### Coffee’s Broader Health Benefits\\nBeyond its impact on liver health, coffee has also been linked to a lower risk of chronic diseases and increased longevity. Research published earlier this year found that regular consumption may support overall well-being by reducing inflammation and improving metabolic function.\\n\\n### What Does This Mean for You?\\nWhile more research is needed to fully understand the mechanisms behind coffee's protective effects, current evidence supports the idea that incorporating moderate amounts of coffee into your daily routine could be beneficial for liver health. Whether you prefer caffeinated or decaf, both options appear to offer similar advantages in terms of liver protection.\\n\\nAs always, it's important to maintain a balanced diet and consult with healthcare professionals before making significant changes to your lifestyle.\", \"claim_to_source\": [{\"claim\": \"Drinking five or more cups of coffee daily reduces liver cancer risk by 47% according to UK Biobank study data.\",\"source_ids\":[\"source:a7913420ce41258bbbdcaf44d19e60bd25480f463d03a153cefa4a43f1f22116:662ecbbf-c771-4650-a668-4660f0f70d13:3\"]},{\"claim\":\"Coffee consumption lowers the risk of cirrhosis by approximately 32% regardless of caffeine content.\",\"source_ids\":[\"source:a7913420ce41258bbbdcaf44d19e60bd25480f463d03a153cefa4a43f1f22116:662ecbbf-c771-4650-a668-4660f0f70d13:3\"]},{\"claim\":\"Decaffeinated coffee provides nearly the same liver protection as regular coffee.\",\"source_ids\":[\"source:a7913420ce41258bbbdcaf44d19e60bd25480f463d03a153cefa4a43f1f22116:662ecbbf-c771-4650-a668-4660f0f70d13:10\"]}]}",
